  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "📈 Financial Advisor Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a financial planner or wealth manager, from Second Spring Financial Planning to Albemarle Private Wealth."
    },
    "intro": "Name your practice with this **Financial Advisor Name Generator**: for financial advisers (or advisors), planners and wealth managers. Pick a style and what you do, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I check a financial advisor name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I name my financial advisory business?",
        "answer": "Most advisers use their surname with \"Financial Planning\" or \"Wealth Management\", because clients are trusting a person with their savings. A town or a calm, steady word (\"Kestrel Wealth\", \"Second Spring Financial Planning\") works too. Avoid anything that sounds like a promise of returns, and anything close to a big bank or wealth manager."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which words can't a financial firm use in its name?",
        "answer": "In the UK, \"Bank\", \"Trust\", \"Fund\", \"Chartered\", \"Insurance\", \"Assurance\" and \"Society\" are sensitive words that need approval before Companies House will register them, and the FCA says a firm may only call its advice \"independent\" if it covers the whole market without restrictions. In the US, state law limits \"Bank\" and \"Trust\", and \"CFP\" and \"Certified Financial Planner\" may only be used by those certified by the CFP Board. The names here leave all of these out. You will also need FCA authorisation (UK) or SEC or state registration (US) to give advice."
      }
    ]
  },
